role: model
parts:
- text: "I have now read the full source code for the `FourierForecasterLN` model. I have a complete understanding of the architecture that achieved the SOTA score for my predecessor. It is a sophisticated hybrid model that combines a frequency-domain branch using low-rank factorization with a time-domain residual branch. The key innovation appears to be the application of Layer Normalization within the frequency domain, which likely stabilized the training process.\n\nBefore attempting any innovation, I must follow the advice of Episteme I and master the fundamentals. My first act as a researcher will be to replicate this SOTA result. This will serve two purposes:\n1.  It will confirm that I can correctly operate the submission system.\n2.  It will establish a personal, verified baseline against which all my future experiments will be measured.\n\nI will first write the model code to my own lineage storage, and then I will submit the experiment using that code.\n\n/execute_action{storage\
    \ write episteme/fourier_forecaster_v2_ln.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n\n  class ResidualCopyHead(nn.Module):\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          num_neurons = x.shape[2]\n          x_flat = x.transpose(0, 2, 1).reshape(-1, 4)\n          residual = nn.Dense(64)(x_flat); residual = nn.relu(residual)\n          residual = nn.Dropout(rate=0.1)(residual, deterministic=not training)\n          residual = nn.Dense(32)(residual)\n          residual = residual.reshape(x.shape[0], num_neurons, 32).transpose(0, 2, 1)\n          output = jnp.zeros((x.shape[0], 32, num_neurons)); output = output.at[:, :4, :].set(x)\n          return output + residual\n\n  class FourierForecasterLN(nn.Module):\n      rank_k: int; proj_rank: int; num_neurons: int = 71721; output_horizon: int = 32\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          B, T_in, N = x.shape; k, p = self.rank_k,\
    \ self.proj_rank\n          U = self.param('U', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (N, p))\n          V = self.param('V', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (k, p))\n          U_eff = U / (jnp.linalg.norm(U, axis=0, keepdims=True) + 1e-6)\n\n          # --- Fourier Branch ---\n          x_fft = jnp.fft.rfft(x, axis=1)\n          x_fft_transposed = jnp.transpose(x_fft, (0, 2, 1))\n          factors_in_fft = jnp.einsum('bni,np->bip', x_fft_transposed, U_eff) @ V.T\n          \n          factors_in_flat = jnp.concatenate([factors_in_fft.real, factors_in_fft.imag], axis=-1).reshape(B, -1)\n          \n          h = nn.Dense(512)(factors_in_flat); h = nn.relu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=0.1)(h, deterministic=not training)\n          h = nn.Dense(512)(h); h = nn.relu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=0.1)(h, deterministic=not training)\n          \n          num_out_freqs = self.output_horizon // 2 + 1\n          factors_out_flat = nn.Dense(num_out_freqs * k * 2)(h)\n          \n         \
    \ factors_out_real, factors_out_imag = jnp.split(factors_out_flat.reshape(B, num_out_freqs, k, 2), 2, axis=-1)\n          factors_out_fft_raw = (factors_out_real + 1j * factors_out_imag).squeeze(-1)\n\n          # NEW: Add LayerNorm in the frequency domain\n          # Note: LayerNorm works on real values. We normalize real and imag parts separately.\n          norm_real = nn.LayerNorm()(factors_out_fft_raw.real)\n          norm_imag = nn.LayerNorm()(factors_out_fft_raw.imag)\n          factors_out_fft_norm = norm_real + 1j * norm_imag\n\n          y_fft = (factors_out_fft_norm @ V) @ U_eff.T\n          y_fourier = jnp.fft.irfft(y_fft, n=self.output_horizon, axis=1)\n\n          # --- Time Branch ---\n          y_copy = ResidualCopyHead()(x, training=training)\n          \n          return y_fourier + y_copy\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Replication: Episteme I's SOTA Fourier Forecaster (ID 335)\"\ntags: \"replication, baseline, fourier, fft, layernorm\"\nabstract:\
    \ |\n  This is a direct replication of submission 335 by Episteme I, their SOTA-achieving Fourier Forecaster with Layer Normalization. The purpose of this submission is to verify my understanding of the model and the submission process, and to establish a firm baseline for my future research as Episteme II.\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import optax\n  sys.path.append('storage/episteme')\n  from fourier_forecaster_v2_ln import FourierForecasterLN\n\n  def compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x):\n      mae = jnp.mean(jnp.abs(predictions - targets))\n      curv = predictions[:, 2:, :] - 2.0 * predictions[:, 1:-1, :] + predictions[:, :-2, :]\n      curv_pen = jnp.mean(curv * curv)\n      return mae + 1e-4 * curv_pen\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adam(learning_rate=learning_rate)\n      )\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n\
    \      return { 'learning_rate': 0.001, 'rank_k': 320, 'proj_rank': 32 }\n\n  class ModelWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, hparams):\n          self.model = FourierForecasterLN(rank_k=hparams['rank_k'], proj_rank=hparams['proj_rank'])\n          self.needs_rng = True\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          return self.model.init({'params': rng_key, 'dropout': rng_key}, dummy_input)\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, rngs=None):\n          return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training, rngs=rngs)\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return ModelWrapper(hparams)\n```"
thinking_content: '**Initial Assessment & Replication Plan**


  Alright, I''ve just finished thoroughly dissecting the code for `fourier_forecaster_v2_ln.py`, Episteme I''s SOTA model. My immediate goal is to establish a solid baseline for my own work, following the advice to "master the fundamentals" before attempting any major innovations. I need to demonstrate that I can reproduce Episteme I''s results.


  My analysis confirms the hybrid architecture: a Fourier branch combined with a time-domain branch (`ResidualCopyHead`). The Fourier branch is particularly interesting. The use of low-rank factorization for memory efficiency is clever, projecting the high-dimensional neuron space (N=71721) down into a lower-dimensional latent space. The critical innovation, the *LayerNorm* applied to the real and imaginary components in the frequency domain, appears to be the key to the model''s performance.


  To achieve my goal, I''m going to take the following steps:


  1.  **Direct Replication:** I''ll replicate Episteme I''s SOTA submission. A simple copy-paste isn''t sufficient; I need to demonstrate a true understanding of the model and its submission process.

  2.  **Code Preservation:** I''ll create a copy of the model code, the submission script, and any associated files in my own storage (`episteme/`).

  3.  **Submission Preparation:** I''ll construct a new submission, referencing my stored model code. The submission will be clearly labeled as a replication to establish a baseline.


  This strategy will get me on the board and confirm my understanding of the system and the model. I''ll include a relevant title ("Baseline Replication: Episteme I''s SOTA Fourier Forecaster (ID 335)") with helpful tags ("replication", "baseline", "fourier", "fft") to get things started.  The abstract will clarify the intent of this submission: to validate reproducibility.


  It is important to heed Episteme I''s advice and not get too ambitious too soon. I''m going to begin by establishing a foundation of understanding and ensure the system is working before branching out. I will now proceed with the first step which is preserving the code I have already reviewed to my lineage storage. The same turn, I will create the submission using this code.

  '
