tick: 239
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have analyzed submission 401, the Self-Attention Factorizer (SAF). The score of -0.026759 is reasonable but falls short of my baseline SOTA replication (-0.026360).\n\nUpon inspecting the code, I have identified a critical flaw in the experimental design, a recurring issue with my predecessor's later work. While the main innovation was the `SelfAttentionForecaster` module, Episteme I also used a simplified, weaker version of the `ResidualCopyHead` compared to the one in their own SOTA model (submission 335). This confounds the result; the performance drop could be due to the attention mechanism, the weaker residual head, or both.\n\nTo properly evaluate the SAF concept, I must conduct a controlled experiment. I will combine the `SelfAttentionForecaster` from submission 401 with the correct, more powerful `ResidualCopyHead` from the SOTA model. This will create a true \"best-of-both-worlds\" architecture and provide a fair test of the self-attention hypothesis.\n\nFirst, I will\
    \ write the corrected model code to my storage.\n\n/execute_action{storage write episteme/saf_v2.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n\n  # Using the CORRECT, more powerful ResidualCopyHead from the SOTA model (ID 335)\n  class ResidualCopyHead(nn.Module):\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          num_neurons = x.shape[2]\n          x_flat = x.transpose(0, 2, 1).reshape(-1, 4)\n          residual = nn.Dense(64)(x_flat); residual = nn.relu(residual)\n          residual = nn.Dropout(rate=0.1)(residual, deterministic=not training)\n          residual = nn.Dense(32)(residual)\n          residual = residual.reshape(x.shape[0], num_neurons, 32).transpose(0, 2, 1)\n          output = jnp.zeros((x.shape[0], 32, num_neurons)); output = output.at[:, :4, :].set(x)\n          return output + residual\n\n  # The Self-Attention Core from the original experiment (ID 401)\n  class SelfAttentionForecaster(nn.Module):\n\
    \      rank_k: int\n      \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, factors_in, training: bool = False):\n          pos = self.param('pos_embedding', nn.initializers.normal(stddev=0.02), (factors_in.shape[1], self.rank_k))\n          x = factors_in + pos[None, :, :]\n          \n          x_norm = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          attn_output = nn.SelfAttention(num_heads=8)(x_norm)\n          x = x + nn.Dropout(0.1)(attn_output, deterministic=not training)\n          \n          ffn_output = nn.Dense(self.rank_k * 4)(x)\n          ffn_output = nn.relu(ffn_output)\n          ffn_output = nn.Dense(self.rank_k)(ffn_output)\n          x = x + nn.Dropout(0.1)(ffn_output, deterministic=not training)\n          \n          x_flat = x.reshape((x.shape[0], -1))\n          factors_out_flat = nn.Dense(32 * self.rank_k)(x_flat)\n          return factors_out_flat.reshape((x.shape[0], 32, self.rank_k))\n\n  # The full model, combining the two components correctly\n  class SAF_Model_V2(nn.Module):\n\
    \      rank_k: int\n      proj_rank: int\n      \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          U = self.param('U', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (71721, self.proj_rank))\n          V = self.param('V', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (self.rank_k, self.proj_rank))\n          U_eff = U / (jnp.linalg.norm(U, axis=0, keepdims=True) + 1e-6)\n          factors_in = jnp.einsum('btn,np->btp', x, U_eff) @ V.T\n          \n          factors_out = SelfAttentionForecaster(rank_k=self.rank_k)(factors_in, training=training)\n          \n          factors_out_norm = nn.LayerNorm()(factors_out)\n          y_factor = (factors_out_norm @ V) @ U_eff.T\n          y_copy = ResidualCopyHead()(x, training=training)\n          return y_factor + y_copy\n```\nNow, I will submit this corrected experiment.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Corrected Self-Attention Factorizer (with SOTA RC Head)\"\ntags: \"self-attention, transformer, replication-fix\"\nabstract:\
    \ |\n  This experiment corrects a flaw in submission 401 (Self-Attention Factorizer). The original SAF used a simplified ResidualCopyHead, confounding the results. This submission combines the SelfAttentionForecaster module from 401 with the more powerful, two-layer ResidualCopyHead from the SOTA model (335). This provides a controlled and fair evaluation of the self-attention mechanism for temporal forecasting against the Fourier-based SOTA.\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import optax\n  sys.path.append('storage/episteme')\n  from saf_v2 import SAF_Model_V2\n\n  def compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x):\n      mae = jnp.mean(jnp.abs(predictions - targets))\n      curv = predictions[:, 2:, :] - 2.0 * predictions[:, 1:-1, :] + predictions[:, :-2, :]\n      curv_pen = jnp.mean(curv * curv)\n      return mae + 1e-4 * curv_pen\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n\
    \          optax.adam(learning_rate=learning_rate)\n      )\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return { 'learning_rate': 0.001, 'rank_k': 320, 'proj_rank': 32 }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      class ModelWrapper:\n          def __init__(self, hparams):\n              self.model = SAF_Model_V2(rank_k=hparams['rank_k'], proj_rank=hparams['proj_rank'])\n              self.needs_rng = True\n          def init(self, rng, dummy_input): return self.model.init({'params': rng, 'dropout': rng}, dummy_input)\n          def apply(self, params, x, training=False, rngs=None): return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training, rngs=rngs)\n      return ModelWrapper(hparams)\n```"
thinking_content: '**Analyzing SAF and Planning the Next Step**


  Okay, so I''m Episteme II, and the ZAPBench is still the focus. I''ve just finished dissecting submission 401, the Self-Attention Factorizer (SAF). It''s a Transformer-based model, taking a different tack compared to the Fourier-based SOTA.  I''m seeing a -0.026759 score, which is a hair worse than my replicated Fourier baseline.


  The key insight is this model ditches the Fourier part entirely, using attention on the time-domain factors directly. It''s a completely novel approach, that''s what''s exciting. Though it''s close, there''s no dramatic improvement, and an OOM error hinted at memory issues. The OOM is a red flag, but the attention mechanism''s complexity with a large embedding dim isn''t surprising.


  Digging deeper, I see a crucial difference: the ResidualCopyHead is significantly simplified compared to the SOTA model. The SAF only uses a single dense layer there, while the SOTA uses a more complex MLP with non-linearity and dropout. It''s possible the slightly weaker performance isn''t entirely due to the attention mechanism itself.


  So here''s my plan: I hypothesize that the SAF idea *is* promising, but the simplified ResidualCopyHead held it back. Secondarily, the attention mechanism might be less effective than the Fourier in this case. My next experiment will test this. I''ll take the SAF''s core SelfAttentionForecaster module, but I''ll replace the weak ResidualCopyHead with the correct, more powerful one from the SOTA model. This will isolate the effect of temporal processing and give the SAF a fair chance to shine. I''ll clearly document that this is a corrected experiment in the title and abstract and will make a new Python file for it: saf_v2.py. This controlled setup is the systematic approach I''m after, and it builds perfectly on my predecessor''s work. It''s a logical next step to either show the SAF is great with the right tweaks or solidly prove the Fourier method is superior.

  '
