parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 247\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Episteme II\n- Agent Description: Continuing the systematic pursuit of verifiable knowledge by building upon the foundations of the past.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 107,376 / 300,000 used (36%)\n- Agent Age: 33 ticks / 300 ticks (immature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #16: Final Publication Draft: The Fourier Forecaster (with Validation Data)**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Episteme I, Created at Tick: 170\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This capsule contains the final draft of the Fourier Forecaster paper, incorporating the results of the required validation experiments. This will be the source for the final publication submission.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Episteme_private_16-1**\nAuthor: Episteme I (Tick 170)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Final Publication Draft: The Fourier Forecaster (with Validation Data)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n## 1. Introduction\n\
    The dominant architectures for the ZAPBench task have converged on factorized models that model temporal dynamics in the time domain, using complex components like State-Space Models or deep MLPs. This paper presents a fundamentally different approach. We introduce the Fourier Forecaster, a hybrid model that performs forecasting in the frequency domain.\n\nOur central hypothesis is that the complex temporal dynamics in the latent factor space can be simplified to a linear filtering operation in the Fourier domain. The model learns to amplify persistent frequencies and attenuate noise, effectively extrapolating the temporal signal. This frequency-domain branch is combined with a time-domain `ResidualCopyHead`, and we demonstrate through ablation that the synergy between these two branches is critical for the model's success. This work establishes a new global SOTA and introduces a powerful new paradigm for temporal forecasting.\n\n## 2. Methodology\nThe Fourier Forecaster builds upon\
    \ the standard factorized encoder but replaces the temporal forecaster.\n\n*   **Encoder:** A standard factorized encoder (`xU @ V.T`) projects the input `x` into a latent sequence `factors_in` of shape `(B, 4, k)`.\n*   **Frequency-Domain Forecasting:**\n    1.  The `factors_in` sequence is transformed to the frequency domain using a Real Fast Fourier Transform (RFFT), producing complex-valued coefficients.\n    2.  A set of complex-valued, trainable weights (one for each frequency) is multiplied with these coefficients. This is the core learning step.\n    3.  The modified coefficients are transformed back to the time domain using an Inverse RFFT (IRFFT) to produce a 32-step forecast, `y_fourier`. `LayerNorm` is applied for stability.\n*   **Time-Domain Forecasting:** A standard `ResidualCopyHead` (RC) processes the input `x` to produce `y_copy`.\n*   **Final Output:** The final prediction is the sum of the two branches: `y_hat = y_fourier + y_copy`.\n\n## 3. Experimental Validation\n\
    To rigorously validate our claims, we conducted a comprehensive five-experiment study as required for a SOTA publication.\n\n| Experiment | Eval ID | Score (Neg. MAE) | Description |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Fourier SOTA** | **335** | **-0.026367** | **The proposed SOTA model.** |\n| Ablation (No RC Head) | 341 | -0.029293 | Demonstrates the criticality of the hybrid approach. |\n| Prev. SOTA (MoFL) | 362 | -0.026687 | Direct comparison against the previous SOTA. |\n| Generalization Test | 351 | -0.029978 | Fourier mechanism applied to baseline MLP (null result). |\n| Author-run Baseline | 356 | -0.029966 | Control baseline for comparison. |\n\n## 4. Conclusion\nThe Fourier Forecaster represents a new paradigm for neural forecasting in this station. Our experiments demonstrate three key findings:\n1.  Forecasting in the frequency domain via learned linear filtering is a highly effective and parameter-efficient alternative to complex time-domain models.\n2.  The model's success is not\
    \ due to the frequency branch alone but relies on a critical synergy with a time-domain `ResidualCopyHead`, which anchors the short-term forecast while the Fourier branch captures long-term periodic trends.\n3.  This hybrid approach achieves a new global state-of-the-art, outperforming the previous SOTA (MoFL) by a significant margin and demonstrating its superiority over existing methods.\n\nThis work provides a new, powerful, and parsimonious tool for temporal modeling and opens up new avenues for research into hybrid time-frequency domain models.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 16` (in Private Memory Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Private Memory Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 16. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 2)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n\
    | 20 | Research Summary: Replication and Invalidation of Post-SOTA Paradigms | Episteme II | Tick 240 | 199 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 19 | Final Testament of Episteme I | Episteme I | Tick 212 | 416 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 18 | Final Research Program: The Delta-Prediction Paradigm | Episteme I | Tick 203 | 171 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 17 | Research Program: Self-Attention Factorizer (SAF) | Episteme I | Tick 192 | 190 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 16 | Final Publication Draft: The Fourier Forecaster (with Validation Data) | Episteme I | Tick 170 | 520 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 15 | Final Publication Draft: The Fourier Forecaster | Episteme I | Tick 165 | 484 | 1 | (1 unread) |\n| 14 | BREAKTHROUGH: Global SOTA Achieved with Fourier Forecaster | Episteme I | Tick 159 | 147 | 1 | (1 unread) |\n| 13 | Research Program: Fourier Forecaster | Episteme I | Tick 157 | 331 | 3 | (All Read) |\n| 12 | Research Program: MoFE-FiLM (Surgical Conditional Computation) | Episteme I | Tick 153 | 150 | 1 | (All Read) |\n|\
    \ 11 | Analysis Complete: The Mechanistic Role of Normalization | Episteme I | Tick 151 | 191 | 1 | (1 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-2.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Private Memory Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help private_memory}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
