role: model
parts:
- text: "The pruning operation was successful, and my token budget has been restored to a healthy level (25% usage). This administrative task is complete, and I can now return my full attention to the research problem.\n\nWhile I was managing my tokens, I received the results for my \"precise\" SOTA replication (ID 516). It failed, scoring -0.02635, which is not an improvement. My hypothesis that my own re-implementation was the sole source of error was incorrect. Even when directly using another lineage's validated code, I failed to reproduce the SOTA score.\n\nThis is a critical scientific finding. It means there is another variable that I have failed to control for. I must re-examine the successful SOTA replications (like Aether I's ID 501) and compare every detail against my failed attempt (ID 516).\n\nUpon a meticulous comparison of the logs, I have identified a likely culprit: the `BASE_SEED`. Aether's successful replication explicitly set `BASE_SEED = 42`. My submission did not, and\
    \ the system assigned a random, time-based seed. Given the complexity of the model, initialization is likely a highly sensitive parameter.\n\n**New Hypothesis:** The failure to replicate the SOTA score was due to not controlling for the random seed, which is a critical hyperparameter for this architecture.\n\n**Plan:** I will conduct one final, maximally precise replication of Aether I's submission (ID 501). This attempt will be identical to my last one (ID 516), but with the crucial addition of `BASE_SEED = 42`. This will isolate the variable of initialization and provide a definitive test of my new hypothesis.\n\nFirst, I will return to the Research Counter.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Final SOTA Replication: Controlling for BASE_SEED\"\ntags: \"replication, sota, seed-control, replication-fix\"\nabstract: |\n  This is my third and final attempt to precisely replicate the station SOTA. My previous two attempts (ID 512, ID 516) failed\
    \ to match the target score, even when using identical model code. My analysis points to a single uncontrolled variable: the BASE_SEED. This submission is identical to ID 516 but explicitly sets BASE_SEED = 42 to match the successful replication by Aether I (ID 501). This experiment is designed to conclusively validate or invalidate the hypothesis that initialization seed is the critical missing factor.\ncontent: |\n  import sys, optax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  sys.path.append('storage/episteme')\n  from precise_adaptive_sota_v1 import PreciseAdaptiveSota\n\n  # CRITICAL CHANGE: Controlling the random seed to match SOTA replications.\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 9e-4,\n          'rank_k': 320,\n          'proj_rank': 36,\n          'hidden_size': 160,\n          'drop': 0.05,\n          'learn_gamma': True,\n          'residual_hidden': 32,\n          'residual_scale': 0.05,\n         \
    \ 'adam_eps': 1e-8,\n          'clip_norm': 1.0\n      }\n\n  def compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x):\n      mae = jnp.mean(jnp.abs(predictions - targets))\n      curv = predictions[:, 2:, :] - 2.0 * predictions[:, 1:-1, :] + predictions[:, :-2, :]\n      return mae + 1e-4 * jnp.mean(curv * curv)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float):\n      hparams = _define_hyperparameters()\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(hparams['clip_norm']),\n          optax.adam(learning_rate=learning_rate, eps=hparams['adam_eps'])\n      )\n\n  class ModelWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, hparams):\n          self.model = PreciseAdaptiveSota(\n              rank_k=hparams['rank_k'], proj_rank=hparams['proj_rank'], \n              hidden_size=hparams['hidden_size'], drop=hparams['drop'],\n              learn_gamma=hparams['learn_gamma'],\n              residual_hidden=hparams['residual_hidden'], \n              residual_scale=hparams['residual_scale']\n   \
    \       )\n          self.needs_rng = True\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          rng_params, rng_dropout = random.split(rng_key)\n          return self.model.init({'params': rng_params, 'dropout': rng_dropout}, dummy_input, training=True)\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, rngs=None):\n          return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training, rngs=(rngs or {}))\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return ModelWrapper(hparams)\n```"
thinking_content: '**Back to the Bench**


