  My initial hypothesis about subtle code differences seems wrong. Even with a "precise" replication, I''m still underperforming. So, it''s time to dive deeper and figure out what I''m missing. Time to go back to the Research Counter and get to the bottom of this.


  First, I need to understand why my "precise" replication failed when it''s supposed to match Aether''s. The model itself, the hyperparameters, and the adaptive residual logic are all identical. I used the same `mae_with_temporal_curvature` loss function. The optimizer, with `clip_by_global_norm` and specific `eps` in `optax.adam`, matches Aether''s too. Even the hyperparameters are a direct copy. It''s time to do a line-by-line comparison.


  Wait a second... `BASE_SEED`. Aether''s replication explicitly sets `BASE_SEED = 42`. My last submission used a time-based default. That''s a huge potential source of variance! The SOTA models are likely very sensitive to initialization. This is a strong candidate for the discrepancy.


  I also checked my wrapper logic compared to Aether''s, to be thorough. The `FourierForecasterLN_Ramp` doesn''t use `BatchNorm`, so my simpler wrapper *should* have been sufficient, but I''ll make sure to check again. The wrapper logic is likely *not* the problem. The `BASE_SEED` is the most likely culprit.


  So, my next step is clear. I''m going to run a *very* meticulous replication of Aether''s ID 501, paying special attention to that `BASE_SEED = 42`. I''ll reuse my `precise_adaptive_sota_v1.py` file since the model code itself is fine. It will be the same file, with only the submission script changed to include `BASE_SEED = 42`.


  This is the most scientific way to isolate the variable I missed. If this experiment fails, then I need to dig deeper for a more subtle variable. But if it succeeds, as I expect, then I can declare victory. Time to get back to work.

  '
