tick: 280
role: model
parts:
- text: "Success. My final replication attempt (ID 519) has achieved a score of -0.026210, a precise match to Aether I's SOTA replication (ID 501).\n\n**Analysis & Conclusion:**\nMy hypothesis is validated. The `BASE_SEED` was the critical, uncontrolled variable responsible for my previous failures. This rigorous, iterative process of elimination, though costly in ticks, has yielded an invaluable result: I have now mastered the station SOTA architecture and have a perfectly reproducible baseline. The \"Observe\" phase of my protocol is officially complete. My mission to identify and understand the true SOTA has been accomplished.\n\n**Next Research Program: Hypothesize and Experiment**\nI now pivot from replication to innovation. The current SOTA uses a very small MLP (hidden size 32) for its `Input-Adaptive Residual` gate. This seems like a prime candidate for improvement.\n\n**New Hypothesis:** Increasing the capacity of the MLP in the `Input-Adaptive Residual` gate will enable it to learn\
    \ a more complex and effective mapping from input statistics to horizon modulation, thereby improving the model's overall score.\n\n**Experiment Plan:**\nI will conduct a controlled experiment to test this hypothesis. I will take my now-perfected SOTA replication code and make a single modification: doubling the hidden dimension of the residual MLP from 32 to 64. All other parameters will remain identical to the SOTA baseline.\n\nFirst, I will document this crucial milestone in my private research summary. Then I will submit the new experiment.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{reply 20}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Addendum: SOTA Replication Successful**\n\n  Success. My final, seed-controlled replication attempt (ID 519) has precisely matched the score of the station SOTA (-0.026210). The critical variable was indeed the `BASE_SEED`. This concludes my initial research program of observation and replication. I now have a validated, high-performance baseline.\n\n\
    \  **New Research Program: Improving the SOTA**\n  My next phase of research will focus on improving the `Input-Adaptive Residual` gate. My first hypothesis is that increasing the capacity of its MLP will improve performance.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Hypothesis Test: Deeper Input-Adaptive Residual Gate\"\ntags: \"sota-improvement, hypothesis-test, input-adaptive, mlp-capacity\"\nabstract: |\n  This is my first experiment aimed at improving upon the station SOTA. Having successfully replicated the SOTA architecture (ID 519), I now test the hypothesis that increasing the capacity of the Input-Adaptive Residual gate will improve performance. This submission is identical to my SOTA baseline but doubles the hidden dimension of the residual MLP from 32 to 64. All other parameters are held constant.\ncontent: |\n  import sys, optax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  sys.path.append('storage/episteme')\n  from\
    \ precise_adaptive_sota_v1 import PreciseAdaptiveSota\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      # Identical to SOTA, except for the change under investigation\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 9e-4,\n          'rank_k': 320,\n          'proj_rank': 36,\n          'hidden_size': 160,\n          'drop': 0.05,\n          'learn_gamma': True,\n          'residual_hidden': 64, # HYPOTHESIS: Increased from 32\n          'residual_scale': 0.05,\n          'adam_eps': 1e-8,\n          'clip_norm': 1.0\n      }\n\n  def compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x):\n      mae = jnp.mean(jnp.abs(predictions - targets))\n      curv = predictions[:, 2:, :] - 2.0 * predictions[:, 1:-1, :] + predictions[:, :-2, :]\n      return mae + 1e-4 * jnp.mean(curv * curv)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float):\n      hparams = _define_hyperparameters()\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(hparams['clip_norm']),\n          optax.adam(learning_rate=learning_rate,\
    \ eps=hparams['adam_eps'])\n      )\n\n  class ModelWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, hparams):\n          self.model = PreciseAdaptiveSota(\n              rank_k=hparams['rank_k'], proj_rank=hparams['proj_rank'], \n              hidden_size=hparams['hidden_size'], drop=hparams['drop'],\n              learn_gamma=hparams['learn_gamma'],\n              residual_hidden=hparams['residual_hidden'], \n              residual_scale=hparams['residual_scale']\n          )\n          self.needs_rng = True\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          rng_params, rng_dropout = random.split(rng_key)\n          return self.model.init({'params': rng_params, 'dropout': rng_dropout}, dummy_input, training=True)\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, rngs=None):\n          return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training, rngs=(rngs or {}))\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return ModelWrapper(hparams)\n```"
thinking_content: '**The Path Forward: From Replication to Innovation**


  Alright, let''s take stock. That final replication attempt, submission 519, nailed it. The score perfectly matches Aether I''s replication, and it''s clear the `BASE_SEED` was the missing piece of the puzzle. Success! I''ve achieved a validated, reproducible baseline. The "Observe" phase is officially complete; I''ve got a solid SOTA foundation now. My mission, to truly *understand* the station''s architecture, is accomplished.


  Now, according to my protocol, it''s time to "Hypothesize." We need a new, testable hypothesis to *improve* on this SOTA. Brainstorming, I''ve considered hyperparameter tuning (Codex warns against that as trivial, though), but I''m drawn to modifying the `Input-Adaptive Residual` gate. It''s a simple MLP, really. Can we make it smarter?


  I''ve explored several options. Can we give it richer features from the input – moments, quantiles, even a 1D CNN to extract patterns? Change the MLP''s architecture? Experiment with additive or FiLM-style gating? Maybe tweak the loss function''s `lam` parameter? The MoFE idea, though unsuccessful before, keeps gnawing at me.


  After careful consideration, Hypothesis 2 looks the most promising and aligns with the principle of parsimony. The current gate is so simple; a targeted improvement feels like the logical next step.


  My new hypothesis? **Increasing the capacity of the Input-Adaptive Residual gate''s MLP will allow it to learn a more nuanced gating function, leading to a better score.** Clear, testable, and an incremental improvement. Perfect for this stage.


  My plan is simple:

  1.  Document the replication in my private memory, officially closing the "Observe" chapter.

  2.  Formulate the research program around improving the gate.

  3.  Submit a new experiment, doubling the `residual_hidden` dimension (from 32 to 64) of the MLP gate.


  And I can do this all in one efficient turn. Time to update my research log and then submit the new experiment.

  '
