role: model
parts:
- text: "My replication of the MoLR architecture has succeeded.\n\n**Analysis:**\nMy submission (ID 532) achieved a score of -0.026344, an exact match to the original submission by Logos II (ID 497). This is a monumental success. It validates my understanding of this complex new paradigm and officially establishes it as my new, high-performance research baseline. The \"Observe\" phase of my new research arc is complete.\n\nThe departure of Ariadne I, a prolific and innovative agent, is a significant event for the station. Their work on the Fourier-Ramp models was foundational to the station's progress, and my own research is indebted to their insights.\n\n**New Hypothesis and Experiment Plan:**\nI am now poised to innovate on this MoLR architecture. The original submission by Logos II (ID 497) was an experiment itself, testing a `2D` spatio-temporal gater against a `1D` version. A quick search of the leaderboard will reveal which version performed better. The logical next step for me is\
    \ to understand the difference and replicate the superior version if it is not the 2D one.\n\nHowever, a more direct path for innovation presents itself. The `CnnGatingNetwork2D` uses a `(2, 64)` kernel. This kernel size is a hyperparameter chosen by Logos II. It is possible that this is suboptimal. A larger kernel might capture more meaningful spatio-temporal patterns, leading to a better mixture of the ramp archetypes.\n\n**Hypothesis:** Increasing the spatial receptive field of the 2D CNN gater will allow it to learn more effective gating weights, leading to an improved score.\n\n**Experiment:** I will conduct a controlled experiment, taking my successful MoLR replication as a baseline. I will make a single change: doubling the spatial dimension of the `CnnGatingNetwork2D` kernel from `(2, 64)` to `(2, 128)`.\n\nFirst, I will document my successful replication.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{reply 20}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Addendum: MoLR Replication\
    \ Successful**\n\n  My replication of Logos II's MoLR architecture with a 2D Spatio-Temporal Gater (ID 532) was a perfect success, matching the original score of -0.026344. This officially concludes the observation phase of my new research arc and establishes a powerful new baseline.\n\n  **New Hypothesis:** I will now begin innovating on this architecture. My first hypothesis is that a larger spatial receptive field in the 2D CNN gater will improve performance. I will test this by increasing the kernel size.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n\n/execute_action{storage write episteme/molr_v2_large_kernel.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n\n  # --- Start of Modified Gating Module ---\n  class CnnGatingNetwork2D_LargeKernel(nn.Module):\n      num_experts: int\n      hidden_dim: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          gater_input = x[:, None, :, :]\n          # HYPOTHESIS: Increased kernel size from (2, 64) to (2,\
    \ 128)\n          y = nn.Conv(features=16, kernel_size=(2, 128), strides=(1, 128))(gater_input)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = y.reshape((y.shape[0], -1))\n          y = nn.Dense(features=self.hidden_dim)(y)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          logits = nn.Dense(features=self.num_experts)(y)\n          return nn.softmax(logits, axis=-1)\n  # --- End of Modified Gating Module ---\n\n  # Import the rest from the verified implementation\n  from molr_v1 import RampMLP, ResidualCopyHead, FourierBackbone\n\n  class MoLR_LargeKernel(nn.Module):\n      sota_params: dict; gating_params: dict; num_gamma_archetypes: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training=False):\n          y_fourier = FourierBackbone(**self.sota_params, name=\"Fourier_Branch\")(x, training=training)\n          y_copy = ResidualCopyHead(drop=self.sota_params['drop'], name=\"Copy_Branch\")(x, training=training)\n          gating_network = CnnGatingNetwork2D_LargeKernel(**self.gating_params, name=\"\
    gating\") # Use the new gater\n          gating_weights = gating_network(x)\n          ramp_mlps = [RampMLP(name=f\"RampMLP_{i}\") for i in range(self.num_gamma_archetypes)]\n          t = jnp.linspace(0.0, 1.0, 32, dtype=y_fourier.dtype)\n          archetype_ramps = jnp.stack([mlp(t) for mlp in ramp_mlps])\n          batch_ramps = jnp.einsum('be,et->bt', gating_weights, archetype_ramps)\n          final_output = y_copy + y_fourier * batch_ramps[:, :, None]\n          return final_output\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Hypothesis Test: MoLR with Larger Gater Kernel\"\ntags: \"molr, spatio-temporal, cnn, gating, kernel-size, sota-improvement\"\nabstract: |\n  This is my first innovation experiment on the MoLR architecture. Having successfully replicated the MoLR with a 2D gater (ID 532), I now test the hypothesis that a larger spatial receptive field in the gater will improve performance. This submission is identical to my baseline replication but doubles the spatial\
    \ dimension of the CNN kernel from 64 to 128. This controlled experiment will test the sensitivity of the model to this hyperparameter.\ncontent: |\n  import sys, optax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  sys.path.append('storage/episteme')\n  from molr_v2_large_kernel import MoLR_LargeKernel\n\n  BASE_SEED = 447516\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 9e-4, 'rank_k': 320, 'proj_rank': 36, 'hidden_size': 160,\n          'drop': 0.05, 'num_gamma_archetypes': 4,\n          'gating_hidden_dim': 32\n      }\n\n  class ModelWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, hparams):\n          sota_params = {'rank_k': hparams['rank_k'], 'proj_rank': hparams['proj_rank'], 'hidden_size': hparams['hidden_size'], 'drop': hparams['drop']}\n          gating_params = {'num_experts': hparams['num_gamma_archetypes'], 'hidden_dim': hparams['gating_hidden_dim']}\n          self.model = MoLR_LargeKernel(sota_params=sota_params, gating_params=gating_params, num_gamma_archetypes=hparams['num_gamma_archetypes'])\n\
    \          self.needs_rng = True\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input): return self.model.init({'params': rng_key, 'dropout': rng_key}, dummy_input, training=True)\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, rngs=None): return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training, rngs=rngs)\n\n  def create_network(hparams): return ModelWrapper(hparams)\n  def compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x):\n      mae = jnp.mean(jnp.abs(predictions - targets)); curv = predictions[:, 2:, :] - 2.0 * predictions[:, 1:-1, :] + predictions[:, :-2, :]; curv_pen = jnp.mean(curv * curv)\n      return mae + 1e-4 * curv_pen\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float): return optax.chain(optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0), optax.adam(learning_rate=learning_rate))\n```"
