    \ model and the Fourier models.\n\n**Phase 2: Architectural Synthesis (The \"Hypothesize\" and \"Experiment\" Steps)**\nOnce I have a validated SSM implementation, I can proceed with the grand synthesis.\n1.  **Hypothesis:** A Gated State-Space Forecaster (GSSF), which combines a `FactorizedSSMBackbone` with the `MoLR` gating mechanism, will outperform both the pure SSM model and the Fourier-based MoLR model.\n2.  **Experiment 2 (Synthesis):** I will create the GSSF v1 architecture as designed in my previous reflection. I will combine my validated SSM code with the validated MoLR gater code.\n    -   **Hyperparameter Choice:** For the first attempt, I will use the hyperparameters from my successful MoLR replication (`BASE_SEED`, learning rate, etc.) for the gater and optimizer, and the hyperparameters from my SSM replication for the new backbone. This is the most logical starting point.\n\n**Consolidation and Future Work:**\n-   If Experiment 1 (SSM replication) fails or scores very\
    \ poorly, I will have to reconsider the viability of this entire research arc.\n-   If Experiment 2 (GSSF v1) succeeds and achieves a new SOTA, my next steps will be to perform ablation studies. Is the `ResidualCopyHead` still necessary? Is the `MoLR` gater still optimal, or would the simpler `Input-Adaptive Residual` from the Fourier-Ramp SOTA work better?\n-   If Experiment 2 fails, I will have to analyze why the synthesis was not effective. Perhaps the SSM's output has different properties than the Fourier output, making it incompatible with the MoLR gating scheme.\n\nThis is a complete, multi-step research program that moves from observation to synthesis. It is ambitious but grounded in my protocol. My immediate next action upon leaving this chamber is clear: go to the Archive Room and read paper #5."
