tick: 313
role: model
parts:
- text: "`Final Plan: The Modular Forecaster Research Program`\n\n**Overarching Goal:** To break the station's local optimum by abandoning the dominant paradigm of global, factorized forecasting and instead developing a new class of models based on the principle of heterogeneous, local dynamics.\n\n**Phase 1: Validation of the Modular Principle**\n\n*   **Step 0: Establish a Non-Factorized Baseline.**\n    *   **Action:** Submit a single, large, monolithic `SharedNeuronMLP` trained on all 71,721 neurons simultaneously. This will serve as the primary baseline for this research program. It will answer the question: \"How well does a simple, non-factorized model perform?\"\n    *   **Rationale:** This controls for architectural class. The goal is to show that a *collection* of small MLPs is better than *one* big MLP, not just that MLPs are better or worse than Fourier models.\n\n*   **Step 1: Data Reconnaissance.**\n    *   **Action:** Go to the Research Counter. Execute `/execute_action{storage\
    \ list system}` to search for any files containing neuron spatial coordinates or brain region annotations.\n    *   **Rationale:** The existence of spatial data provides the most interpretable basis for clustering.\n\n*   **Step 2: First Modular Experiment (Static Modular Forecaster - SMF).**\n    *   **Action:** Submit the first SMF model. The implementation will use a gather-process-scatter logic with independent `SharedNeuronMLP` experts for `k` clusters.\n    *   **Clustering Method:**\n        *   **Path A (Spatial Data Exists):** Use k-means on neuron coordinates. Title: \"SMF v1: Spatial Clustering\".\n        *   **Path B (No Spatial Data):** First, run a `test()` submission to compute and save the mean activity of each neuron. Then, use k-means on these statistics for clustering. Title: \"SMF v1: Statistical Clustering\".\n    *   **Hypothesis:** The SMF model will outperform the monolithic MLP baseline from Step 0.\n\n**Contingency & Future Phases:**\n\n*   **If SMF succeeds\
    \ (outperforms monolithic MLP):** This is a major breakthrough. It validates the principle of modularity.\n    *   **Phase 2:** Begin work on a **Dynamic Modular Forecaster (DMF)**. Introduce a gating network that learns soft, input-dependent cluster assignments for each neuron, feeding them to the appropriate expert. This moves from static to dynamic heterogeneity.\n    *   **Phase 3:** Explore **Hierarchical & Hybrid Models**. Could the outputs of the modular experts be fed into a global (perhaps even Fourier-based) model to capture both local and global dynamics?\n\n*   **If SMF fails:** This would be a significant blow to the modularity hypothesis.\n    *   **Analysis:** I would first need to rigorously question the implementation. Was the clustering method poor? Were the experts too simple? Was `k` chosen badly?\n    *   **Follow-up:** I would likely test a different static clustering method (e.g., based on variance instead of mean) as a sanity check. If that also fails, I may need\
    \ to conclude that neuron-level interactions are more complex than simple clustering can capture, and a new paradigm is needed.\n\nThis plan is systematic, grounded in a clear conceptual break from past approaches, and includes robust controls and contingency plans. I am ready to begin. My reflection is complete."
