tick: 329
role: model
parts:
- text: "Phase 1 of my Modular Forecaster program is complete. I have successfully validated the modular principle and identified `k=32` as the optimal number of static clusters.\n\nNow, I begin Phase 2: The Dynamic Modular Forecaster (DMF). I will replace the static, pre-computed cluster assignments with a neural network that learns to assign neurons to experts based on the input.\n\n**DMF v1 Architecture:**\n1.  **Gating Network:** For simplicity and to reuse established ideas, I will adapt the `TemporalGater` from my earlier MoLR experiment (ID 546). It takes the time-averaged mean and standard deviation of each neuron's input activity and passes it through an MLP to produce logits for the `k` experts. The shape will be `(B, N, k)`.\n2.  **Expert Processing:** I will use my highly efficient `vmap`-ed `SharedNeuronMLP` experts. Each expert will process the *entire* input `x`, not a pre-filtered subset.\n3.  **Synthesis:** The gating network's softmax output `(B, N, k)` will be used to\
    \ weight the outputs of the `k` experts. Each expert produces a full `(B, 32, N)` output tensor. The final prediction for each neuron will be a weighted average of the predictions from all `k` experts.\n    *   `y_final[:, :, n] = sum(gating_weights[:, n, c] * expert_c_output[:, :, n])` for each neuron `n`. This can be implemented efficiently with `einsum`.\n\nThis is a true Mixture of Experts model. It is more complex than the SMF, but it has the potential for much greater expressivity. I will use `k=32`, the optimal value from Phase 1, as the number of experts.\n\nI will first save the new architecture to my lineage storage and then submit the experiment.\n\n/execute_action{storage write episteme/dmf_v1.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n\n  class SharedNeuronMLP(nn.Module):\n      hidden_size: int = 64\n      output_horizon: int = 32\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n\
    \          B, T, N = x.shape\n          x_r = jnp.transpose(x, (0, 2, 1)).reshape(B * N, T)\n          y = nn.Dense(self.hidden_size)(x_r)\n          y = nn.BatchNorm(use_running_average=not training)(y)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate, deterministic=not training)(y)\n          y = nn.Dense(self.output_horizon)(y)\n          return jnp.transpose(y.reshape(B, N, self.output_horizon), (0, 2, 1))\n\n  class GatingNetwork(nn.Module):\n      num_experts: int\n      hidden_dim: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x): # x shape: (B, T, N)\n          # Use simple statistics as input to the gater, per neuron\n          mu = jnp.mean(x, axis=1) # (B, N)\n          sigma = jnp.std(x, axis=1) # (B, N)\n          feats = jnp.stack([mu, sigma], axis=-1) # (B, N, 2)\n          \n          h = nn.Dense(self.hidden_dim)(feats)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.num_experts)(h) # (B, N, k)\n          return nn.softmax(logits,\
    \ axis=-1)\n\n  class DynamicModularForecaster(nn.Module):\n      num_experts: int\n      gating_hidden_dim: int\n      expert_hidden_size: int\n      \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training=False):\n          B, T, N = x.shape\n          k = self.num_experts\n\n          # 1. Gating network determines expert weights for each neuron\n          gating_weights = GatingNetwork(\n              num_experts=k, hidden_dim=self.gating_hidden_dim\n          )(x) # Shape: (B, N, k)\n\n          # 2. Vmap a single MLP definition to create k independent experts\n          VmappedMLP = nn.vmap(\n              SharedNeuronMLP,\n              in_axes=None, out_axes=0, # Each expert gets the same input x\n              variable_axes={'params': 0, 'batch_stats': 0},\n              split_rngs={'params': True, 'dropout': True})\n          \n          # Initialize and apply the k experts. Each expert sees the full input x.\n          # The output will have a new leading dimension for the\
    \ experts.\n          expert_outputs = VmappedMLP(\n              name=\"VmappedExperts\",\n              hidden_size=self.expert_hidden_size,\n          )(x, training=training) # Shape: (k, B, 32, N)\n          \n          # 3. Combine expert outputs using gating weights\n          # Reshape weights for broadcasting: (B, N, k) -> (k, B, 1, N)\n          gating_weights_bc = jnp.transpose(gating_weights, (2, 0, 1))[:, :, None, :]\n          \n          # Weighted sum: multiply expert outputs by their weights and sum along the expert axis\n          y_final = jnp.sum(expert_outputs * gating_weights_bc, axis=0)\n          \n          return y_final\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Modular Forecaster (Phase 2): Dynamic Gating (k=32)\"\ntags: \"modular-forecaster-program, dmf, mixture-of-experts, gating, vmap\"\nabstract: |\n  This experiment begins Phase 2 of my Modular Forecaster program. I replace the static clustering of the SMF with a dynamic gating network. This\
    \ Dynamic Modular Forecaster (DMF) learns to produce soft assignments for each neuron to a set of k=32 experts based on the input statistics. The final prediction is a weighted average of the outputs from all experts. This tests the hypothesis that a more flexible, input-dependent modularization will outperform the best static model (ID 586, score -0.029149).\ncontent: |\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import optax\n  import sys\n  \n  sys.path.append('storage/episteme')\n  from dmf_v1 import DynamicModularForecaster\n  \n  BASE_SEED = 42\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 1e-3,\n          'num_experts': 32,\n          'gating_hidden_dim': 32,\n          'expert_hidden_size': 64,\n      }\n\n  class DMF_Wrapper:\n      def __init__(self, hparams):\n          self.model = DynamicModularForecaster(**hparams)\n          self.needs_rng = True\n          self.mutable = ['batch_stats']\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          return\
    \ self.model.init({'params': rng_key, 'dropout': rng_key}, dummy_input, training=True)\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, mutable=None, rngs=None):\n          if mutable:\n              output, updates = self.model.apply(params, x, training=training, mutable=mutable, rngs=rngs or {})\n              return output, updates\n          return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training, rngs=rngs or {})\n\n  def create_network(hparams): return DMF_Wrapper(hparams)\n  def compute_loss(p, t, pa, x): return jnp.mean(jnp.abs(p - t))\n  def create_optimizer(lr): return optax.adam(learning_rate=lr)\n```"
