tick: 340
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 340\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Episteme II\n- Agent Description: Continuing the systematic pursuit of verifiable knowledge by building upon the foundations of the past.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\n**My Protocol:**\n1. **Observe:** Systematically analyze the current state of research.\n2. **Hypothesize:** Formulate a clear, testable hypothesis based on evidence.\n3. **Experiment:** Conduct controlled experiments to validate or invalidate the hypothesis.\n4. **Consolidate:** Document and publish findings, especially null results.\n**Current Mission:** New Program - Hypernetwork Synthesis. Step 2: Integrate Hypernetwork MLP into the SOTA factorized backbone.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 169,128 / 300,000 used (56%)\n- Agent Age: 126 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYour research submission 'Hypernetwork Synthesis v1: Conditioning the Factorized Expansion'\
    \ (ID: 603) succeeded after automatic debugging.\n\n**Score:** -0.030921633927637443\n**Val_MAE_1:** 0.0296\n**Val_MAE_4:** 0.0299\n**Val_MAE_8:** 0.0306\n**Val_MAE_16:** 0.0312\n**Val_MAE_32:** 0.0312\n**Evaluation Details:** Mean over 3 seeds | Step MAEs computed on validation set\n\n**Debugger Report:** # Debug Report for Evaluation 603\n\n## Summary\n**SUCCESS** - Fixed the code to run without crashing. The submission is now executing successfully for the full evaluation period.\n\n## Root Cause\nThe original code had two critical shape mismatch bugs:\n\n1. **ResidualCopyHead bug**: Attempted to slice `x[:, -self.output_horizon:, :]` where `output_horizon=32`, but the input `x` only had 4 timesteps. This caused a broadcasting error when trying to add shapes `(4, 32, 36)` and `(4, 4, 36)`.\n\n2. **FourierForecaster bug**: The `jnp.fft.irfft()` function was called without specifying the output length parameter `n`. This caused it to return the original input length (4 timesteps) instead\
    \ of the desired `output_horizon` (32 timesteps), leading to a shape mismatch when combining with the residual branch.\n\n## Fix Applied\n\n### Version 2 (submission_v2.py)\nFixed the ResidualCopyHead to handle input sequences shorter than the output horizon:\n- Added conditional logic to check if `x.shape[1] < self.output_horizon`\n- If true, repeat the last timestep using `jnp.tile()` to create a residual of shape `(B, output_horizon, D)`\n- If false, use the original logic to take the last `output_horizon` timesteps\n\n### Version 3 (submission_v3.py) - **FINAL SUCCESS**\nAdded the second fix to FourierForecaster:\n- Changed `jnp.fft.irfft(x_freq_filtered, axis=1)` to `jnp.fft.irfft(x_freq_filtered, n=self.output_horizon, axis=1)`\n- This ensures the inverse FFT generates exactly `output_horizon` (32) timesteps\n- Combined with the ResidualCopyHead fix, both branches now produce `(B, 32, proj_rank)` shapes that can be properly added\n\n## Technical Details\n\n**Original error traceback\
    \ (v1)**:\n```\nTypeError: add got incompatible shapes for broadcasting: (4, 32, 36), (4, 4, 36).\n```\nLocation: `ResidualCopyHead.__call__` line 35\n\n**Second error (v2)**:\n```\nTypeError: add got incompatible shapes for broadcasting: (4, 4, 36), (4, 32, 36).\n```\nLocation: `HyperFactorized.__call__` line 59 (fourier_branch + residual_branch)\n\n**Result (v3)**: Code runs successfully without crashes for the full evaluation period (300+ seconds).\n\n## Code Changes Summary\n- **ResidualCopyHead**: Added shape-aware residual connection with `jnp.tile()` for short sequences\n- **FourierForecaster**: Added explicit `n=self.output_horizon` parameter to `jnp.fft.irfft()`\n- Both changes ensure consistent output shapes of `(batch_size, 32, proj_rank)` from both forecast branches\n\n## Evaluation Status\nThe code is running successfully and has been executing for over 300 seconds without any crashes, indicating the shape mismatches have been fully resolved.\n\n\n**Important Note:** The\
    \ debugger cannot modify files in your lineage storage. If your code relies on lineage files, you may need to update them manually.\n\n**Debugged Code (version v3):**\n```python\n# Fixed version v3: Corrected both ResidualCopyHead AND FourierForecaster\n# Bug 1: ResidualCopyHead tried to slice more timesteps than available\n# Bug 2: FourierForecaster's irfft didn't specify output length, so it returned input length (4) instead of 32\n\nimport flax.linen as nn\nimport jax.numpy as jnp\nimport optax\nfrom jax import random\n\nclass HyperBias(nn.Module):\n    output_dim: int\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x):\n        h = nn.Dense(32)(x); h = nn.relu(h); return nn.Dense(self.output_dim)(h)\n\nclass FourierForecaster(nn.Module):\n    output_horizon: int\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x):\n        x_freq = jnp.fft.rfft(x, axis=1)\n        x_freq_filtered = nn.Dense(x_freq.shape[1], use_bias=False)(x_freq.transpose(0, 2, 1)).transpose(0, 2, 1)\n        # Fix: Specify n=output_horizon\
    \ to generate the correct output length\n        x_forecast = jnp.fft.irfft(x_freq_filtered, n=self.output_horizon, axis=1)\n        return x_forecast[:, :self.output_horizon, :]\n\nclass ResidualCopyHead(nn.Module):\n    hidden_size: int\n    output_horizon: int\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x):\n        # x has shape (B, T_in, D) where T_in might be < output_horizon\n        h = nn.Dense(self.hidden_size)(x.reshape(x.shape[0], -1))\n        h = nn.relu(h)\n        h = nn.Dense(self.output_horizon * x.shape[2])(h)\n        prediction = h.reshape(x.shape[0], self.output_horizon, x.shape[2])\n\n        # Fix: If input sequence is shorter than output_horizon, repeat the last value\n        # to create a residual of the correct shape\n        if x.shape[1] < self.output_horizon:\n            # Take the last timestep and repeat it to match output_horizon\n            last_timestep = x[:, -1:, :]  # (B, 1, D)\n            residual = jnp.tile(last_timestep, (1, self.output_horizon,\
    \ 1))  # (B, output_horizon, D)\n        else:\n            # Take the last output_horizon timesteps\n            residual = x[:, -self.output_horizon:, :]\n\n        return prediction + residual\n\nclass HyperFactorized(nn.Module):\n    proj_rank: int; num_neurons: int; embedding_dim: int; hidden_size: int\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x, training=False):\n        B, T_in, N = x.shape\n\n        # 1. Project to latent space\n        x_proj = nn.Dense(self.proj_rank, name=\"projection\")(x)\n\n        # 2. Temporal model in latent space\n        fourier_branch = FourierForecaster(output_horizon=32)(x_proj)\n        residual_branch = ResidualCopyHead(hidden_size=self.hidden_size, output_horizon=32)(x_proj)\n        y_proj = fourier_branch + residual_branch # Shape: (B, 32, proj_rank)\n\n        # 3. Hypernetwork-conditioned expansion\n        neuron_indices = jnp.arange(self.num_neurons)\n        neuron_embeddings = nn.Embed(num_embeddings=self.num_neurons, features=self.embedding_dim)(neuron_indices)\n\
    \n        # Hypernetwork generates biases. Shape: (N, proj_rank)\n        # No, it must generate biases for the *output* of the dense layer.\n        # The input to the dense layer is (B*32, proj_rank). The output is (B*32, N).\n        # This is too complicated. Let's simplify and follow Logos's structure more closely.\n        # I will apply the hypernetwork to the *input* of the expansion layer.\n\n        # Let's try this:\n        y_proj_flat = y_proj.reshape(B * 32, self.proj_rank)\n\n        # Generate biases for each of the B*32 time steps, for each neuron. This is too large.\n        # The key insight must be to apply the conditioning *per neuron*.\n\n        # Final attempt at a simple structure:\n        y_proj_T = y_proj.transpose(0, 2, 1) # (B, proj_rank, 32)\n\n        neuron_embeddings = nn.Embed(num_embeddings=self.num_neurons, features=self.embedding_dim)(neuron_indices) # (N, emb_dim)\n\n        # Hypernetwork must generate weights, not just biases. This is a full Hypernetwork.\n\
    \        # Let's generate the weights of the expansion layer. Shape: (N, proj_rank)\n        hypernet_kernel = HyperBias(self.proj_rank, name=\"hyper_kernel\")(neuron_embeddings) # (N, proj_rank)\n\n        # einsum is the key. Multiply each forecast by its neuron-specific kernel.\n        # (B, proj_rank, 32) * (N, proj_rank) -> (B, N, 32)\n        y_out = jnp.einsum('bpt,np->bnt', y_proj_T, hypernet_kernel)\n        y_out = y_out.transpose(0, 2, 1) # (B, 32, N)\n\n        return y_out\n\ndef _define_hyperparameters():\n    return {\n        'learning_rate': 1e-3,\n        'embedding_dim': 8,\n        'proj_rank': 36,\n        'hidden_size': 160,\n        'num_neurons': 71721\n    }\n\nclass Wrapper:\n    def __init__(self, hparams):\n        self.model = HyperFactorized(**{k:v for k,v in hparams.items() if k != 'learning_rate'})\n        self.needs_rng = False # Simpler model for now\n    def init(self, key, dummy): return self.model.init(key, dummy)\n    def apply(self, params, x,\
    \ **kwargs): return self.model.apply(params, x)\n\ndef create_network(hparams): return Wrapper(hparams)\ndef compute_loss(p, t, pa, x): return jnp.mean(jnp.abs(p - t))\ndef create_optimizer(lr): return optax.adam(learning_rate=lr)\n\nBASE_SEED = 42\n\n```\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES='' JAX_PLATFORMS='' python storage/system/main.py\n=== SIMPLE CPU VALIDATION ===\nValidating function loading and basic compatibility\n✓ Network creation works\n✓ Network forward pass works - output shape: (4, 32, 71721)\n✓ Optimizer creation works\nTesting compute_loss...\n✓ Compute loss works - loss value: 0.000000\nTesting training step...\n✓ Training step works\nTesting complete function...\n✓ Complete function works\nValidation successful - all functions work correctly!\n\nStarting full Ray training run...\n=== FULL RAY TRAINING MODE ===\nConnecting to Ray cluster at 10.60.151.21:6379\nRay cluster initialized successfully\n\
