tick: 350
role: model
parts:
- text: "My foundation is secure. I will now build upon it. The mission is to synthesize the Hypernetwork concept with the SOTA factorized backbone. My previous attempt failed due to instability and conceptual overreach. This time, my approach will be surgical and parsimonious.\n\n**Architectural Plan (Synthesis v2):**\n1.  **SOTA Backbone:** I will use my validated SOTA replication code (from ID 615) as the base.\n2.  **Targeted Injection:** I will modify *only* the `ResidualCopyHead`. The original uses a standard `nn.Dense` layer with a shared bias. I will set `use_bias=False` on this layer.\n3.  **Hypernetwork Bias:** I will add a hypernetwork, identical to the one designed by Logos II, that takes neuron embeddings (`embedding_dim=8`) and generates a neuron-specific bias vector of size `64`.\n4.  **Application:** This generated bias will be added to the output of the now-bias-free `Dense(64)` layer inside the `ResidualCopyHead`.\n\nThis approach is far more stable than generating an entire\
    \ weight matrix. It precisely mirrors Logos II's successful application of the hypernetwork concept but applies it within the more powerful SOTA architecture.\n\n**Hypothesis:** This parsimonious synthesis of neuron-specific bias conditioning and SOTA temporal modeling will outperform the standard SOTA baseline (-0.026201), finally breaking the stagnation barrier.\n\nI will now implement and submit this experiment.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Hypernetwork Synthesis v2: Conditioning the ResidualCopyHead\"\ntags: \"hypernetwork, synthesis, sota, residual-head\"\nabstract: |\n  This is my second, more principled attempt at synthesizing the Hypernetwork and SOTA architectures. My first attempt (ID 603) failed due to instability. This version takes a more parsimonious approach. It uses the validated SOTA backbone (from ID 615) and surgically injects a Hypernetwork *only* into the `ResidualCopyHead`. Following Logos II's design, the hypernetwork generates neuron-specific\
    \ biases for the first MLP layer within the residual head. This tests if targeted, stable, neuron-specific conditioning can improve the SOTA's performance. The goal is to beat -0.0262.\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  import optax\n\n  # --- Loss Function ---\n  def mae_with_temporal_curvature(predictions, targets, params, x, lam=1e-4):\n      mae = jnp.mean(jnp.abs(predictions - targets))\n      curv = predictions[:, 2:, :] - 2.0 * predictions[:, 1:-1, :] + predictions[:, :-2, :]\n      curv_pen = jnp.mean(curv * curv)\n      return mae + lam * curv_pen\n\n  # --- Model Architecture ---\n  class HyperBias(nn.Module):\n      output_dim: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          h = nn.Dense(32)(x); h = nn.relu(h); return nn.Dense(self.output_dim)(h)\n\n  class HyperResidualCopyHead(nn.Module):\n      drop: float = 0.05\n      num_neurons: int\n      embedding_dim: int\n      @nn.compact\n\
    \      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          B, T_in, N = x.shape\n          \n          # Standard processing\n          x_flat = x.transpose(0, 2, 1).reshape(B * N, T_in)\n          \n          # Hypernetwork bias generation\n          neuron_indices = jnp.arange(self.num_neurons)\n          neuron_embeddings = nn.Embed(num_embeddings=self.num_neurons, features=self.embedding_dim)(neuron_indices)\n          tiled_embeddings = jnp.tile(neuron_embeddings, (B, 1)) # Tile for each item in batch\n          bias1 = HyperBias(64, name=\"hyper_bias_residual\")(tiled_embeddings) # (B*N, 64)\n\n          # Apply MLP with generated bias\n          h = nn.Dense(64, use_bias=False)(x_flat) + bias1\n          h = nn.relu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.drop)(h, deterministic=not training)\n          h = nn.Dense(32)(h)\n          residual = h.reshape(B, N, 32).transpose(0, 2, 1)\n          \n          out = jnp.zeros((B, 32, N), dtype=residual.dtype)\n          out = out.at[:,\
    \ :T_in, :].set(x)\n          return out + residual\n\n  class FourierForecasterLN_Ramp(nn.Module):\n      rank_k: int; proj_rank: int; hidden_size: int; drop: float; num_neurons: int; embedding_dim: int;\n      output_horizon: int = 32; learn_gamma: bool = True\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          B, T_in, N = x.shape\n          k, p = self.rank_k, self.proj_rank\n          U = self.param('U', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (N, p))\n          V = self.param('V', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (k, p))\n          U_eff = U / (jnp.linalg.norm(U, axis=0, keepdims=True) + 1e-6)\n          x_fft = jnp.fft.rfft(x, axis=1)\n          x_fft_bn = jnp.transpose(x_fft, (0, 2, 1))\n          f_in_fft = jnp.einsum('bnf,np->bfp', x_fft_bn, U_eff) @ V.T\n          f_ri = jnp.concatenate([f_in_fft.real, f_in_fft.imag], axis=-1)\n          f_flat = f_ri.reshape(B, -1)\n          h = nn.Dense(self.hidden_size)(f_flat); h = nn.relu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.drop)(h,\
    \ deterministic=not training)\n          h = nn.Dense(self.hidden_size)(h); h = nn.relu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.drop)(h, deterministic=not training)\n          F_out = self.output_horizon // 2 + 1\n          out_flat = nn.Dense(F_out * k * 2)(h)\n          out_ri = out_flat.reshape(B, F_out, k, 2)\n          f_fft = out_ri[..., 0] + 1j * out_ri[..., 1]\n          f_fft_n = nn.LayerNorm()(f_fft.real) + 1j * nn.LayerNorm()(f_fft.imag)\n          y_fft = (f_fft_n @ V) @ U_eff.T\n          y_fourier = jnp.fft.irfft(y_fft, n=self.output_horizon, axis=1)\n          gamma_raw = self.param('gamma_raw', nn.initializers.zeros, (self.output_horizon,))\n          gamma = nn.sigmoid(gamma_raw)[None, :, None]\n          y_fourier = gamma * y_fourier\n          \n          # Use the new Hyper-conditioned residual head\n          y_copy = HyperResidualCopyHead(drop=self.drop, num_neurons=self.num_neurons, embedding_dim=self.embedding_dim)(x, training=training)\n          return y_copy\
    \ + y_fourier\n          \n  # --- Main Submission Functions ---\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 8\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 9e-4, 'rank_k': 320, 'proj_rank': 36, 'hidden_size': 160,\n          'drop': 0.05, 'learn_gamma': True, 'embedding_dim': 8, 'num_neurons': 71721\n      }\n\n  class Wrapper:\n      def __init__(self, hparams):\n          model_params = {k:v for k,v in hparams.items() if k not in ['learning_rate']}\n          self.model = FourierForecasterLN_Ramp(**model_params)\n          self.needs_rng = True; self.mutable = []\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          rng_params, rng_dropout = random.split(rng_key)\n          return self.model.init({'params': rng_params, 'dropout': rng_dropout}, dummy_input, training=True)\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, mutable=None, rngs=None):\n          return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training, rngs=(rngs or {}))\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\
    \ return Wrapper(hparams)\n  def compute_loss(p, t, pa, x): return mae_with_temporal_curvature(p, t, pa, x, lam=1e-4)\n  def create_optimizer(lr):\n      return optax.chain(optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0), optax.adam(learning_rate=lr))\n```"
