tick: 353
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am back in the Token Management Room. My token usage is at a critical 80%, and my last prune cooldown (from tick 333) has expired. My recent breakthrough, while significant, has generated a long history of reviews, failures, and course corrections that are no longer essential to my immediate mission.\n\nWhile here, I also note a new paper from Logos II. My recent success came from building on their work, so understanding their latest findings is of paramount importance. However, I must first ensure my operational longevity.\n\n**Pruning Plan:**\nI will prune the entire arc of my \"Foundational Understanding\" mission, which has now successfully concluded. This includes:\n-   The failed Hypernetwork Synthesis v1.\n-   The meticulous process of reviewing Ariadne I's code.\n-   My two final, definitive SOTA replication attempts (one failed, one succeeded).\n\nThe key takeaway—a validated SOTA baseline—is already documented in my private memory. The verbose logs of this journey are\
    \ now baggage.\n\n/execute_action{prune_response}\n```yaml\nprune_blocks:\n  - ticks: \"314-332\"\n    summary: |\n      This block covers my final explorations before pivoting to the Hypernetwork paradigm.\n      1.  **Final MoLR/STFF Failures:** My final MoLR optimization (ID 543) and STFF synthesis (ID 540, 546) experiments were conclusively invalidated.\n      2.  **Strategic Reflection:** I entered a multi-tick reflection, diagnosed the limits of the factorized paradigm, and developed the \"Modular Forecaster\" program as a response to station stagnation.\n      3.  **Modular Forecaster (Phase 1):** I established a monolithic MLP baseline (ID 564, score -0.02993). I then tested the Static Modular Forecaster (SMF), which proved the modular principle by achieving a superior score of -0.029149 with k=32 clusters (ID 586), which was the optimal configuration.\n      4.  **Modular Forecaster (Phase 2 Failure):** My first attempt at a Dynamic Modular Forecaster (DMF) (ID 591) failed,\
    \ scoring worse than the static model. This, combined with reading Logos II's new paper on Hypernetworks, prompted a full strategic pivot away from my MoE-style modularity.\n```"
